meta {
  name: Get Product by ID
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.data.id: eq 6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06
  res.body.data.sku: eq BAG-10157
}

docs {
  `GET /api/v3/products/{identifier}` — fetch a single product by its Mongo
  ObjectId. Confirmed live 2026-09-07 against "David's Dev Account". Reference
  fixture: SKU `BAG-10157`, id `6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06` (pre-existing, read-only).

  ## Identifier: only Mongo ObjectId accepted on v3
  `API V3.md` documents SKU as a valid `{identifier}`. This is **false on v3**:
  passing a SKU returns
  `422 {"errors":[{"name":"ValidationError","description":"Id must be of type
  PydanticObjectId"}]}`. Confirmed on both a pre-existing product and a product
  created within this test session — rules out any "legacy product" theory. Flag
  to Plytix: likely a v1-only behaviour incorrectly carried into v3 docs.
  See "422 — SKU as Identifier" example below.

  ## Response shape
  Returns the full `ProductOutputDto`. Key spec discrepancy: the relationship field
  in the live response is named `product_relationships`, not `relationships` as
  declared in `openapi_pimv3.json`. Two distinct wrong-field behaviours on the
  search endpoint confirm this is a naming issue, not a missing field:
  `_fields=category_ids` returns 400 `HiddenFieldError`; `_fields=relationships`
  is silently dropped with no error.

  ## Undocumented raw-model fields
  A subpath-discovery probe (`GET /products/{id}/any_bogus_path`) returns
  `400 {"errors":[{"name":"ValueError","description":"Field '<path>' not found.
  Available fields: [...]"}]}` listing the internal model fields. For this product:
  `['id','account_id','sku','label','gtin','status','attributes','thumbnail_id',
  'parent_id','num_variations','product_level','product_family_id',
  'product_family_model_id','overwritten_attributes','mark_as_deleted',
  'product_relationships','created','modified','last_context',
  'modified_user_audit','created_user_audit']`. Several of these
  (`product_family_model_id`, `mark_as_deleted`, `last_context`,
  `modified_user_audit`, `created_user_audit`) are absent from
  `openapi_pimv3.json`'s `ProductOutputDto` — ask Plytix whether any are
  intentionally customer-facing. This 400-with-field-list trick is the fastest
  way to discover a resource's real fields in any later phase — use it before
  guessing subpath names from the spec alone.

  ## 422 error envelope shape
  `errors` is a flat `[{name, description}]` array — distinct from:
  - The 401 auth error shape: `error.msg` / nested `errors[].field`.
  - FastAPI's default `HTTPValidationError`: `detail[{loc,msg,type}]`.
  Plytix wraps all validation errors in its own custom envelope.
}

example {
  name: 200 - Fetch by Mongo ObjectId
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-07. Full ProductOutputDto shape. Note: relationship field is product_relationships (not relationships per spec). categories and assets are not expanded by default — use GET /products/{id}/{path} subpath requests for those.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06
    method: get
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "id": "6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06",
            "account_id": "6a38f5a5b22478f30bfd755c",
            "sku": "BAG-10157",
            "label": "Bag 10157",
            "gtin": null,
            "status": "Completed",
            "thumbnail_id": "6a38f5e1c3d65a5f868b18a5",
            "attributes": {
              "brand": "Plytix",
              "color": "Black",
              "amazon_ready": 85.71,
              "basic_information_completeness": 100.0,
              "website_ready": 100.0
            },
            "product_relationships": {
              "related_products": [
                {
                  "quantity": 1,
                  "last_modified": "2026-01-15T10:00:00.000000+00:00"
                }
              ]
            },
            "parent_id": null,
            "num_variations": 0,
            "product_level": 0,
            "product_type": "SINGLE",
            "product_family_id": "6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186e",
            "product_family_model_id": null,
            "overwritten_attributes": [],
            "created": "2024-06-01T08:00:00.000000+00:00",
            "modified": "2026-09-01T12:00:00.000000+00:00",
            "created_user_audit": {
              "type": "UI",
              "user_email": "david@example.com",
              "user_id": "6a38f5d5c3d65a5f868b186a"
            },
            "modified_user_audit": {
              "type": "UI",
              "user_email": "david@example.com",
              "user_id": "6a38f5d5c3d65a5f868b186a"
            }
          },
          "errors": []
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}

example {
  name: 200 - _fields Ignored, Full Rich Product Returned
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-22 (re-confirms quirk #24 -- _fields is silently ignored on single-resource GET, only works on the search endpoint). Requesting _fields=sku&_fields=status still returns the complete ProductOutputDto with its full real attributes map (BAG-10157 has ~50 populated attributes in this account) and product_relationships. Also demonstrates a real, fully-populated attributes map for OpenAPI-example purposes -- more representative than the sparser scratch-fixture examples elsewhere in this file. Note: product_relationships here renders as a flat array of {relationship_id, relationship_label, related_products} entries -- a different shape than the nested {"related_products": [...]} object shown in the "Success - Fetch by Mongo ObjectId" example above (both captured against the same live product on different dates); worth flagging to Plytix as a possible in-flight shape change on this field.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06?_fields=sku&_fields=status
    method: get
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "id": "6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06",
            "account_id": "6a38f5a5b22478f30bfd755c",
            "sku": "BAG-10157",
            "label": "Plytix Backpack",
            "gtin": "9780201379624",
            "status": "Completed",
            "attributes": {
              "brand": "Plytix",
              "color": ["Black"],
              "material": ["Nylon", "Polyester"],
              "price": 29.99,
              "product_title": "Plytix Backpack",
              "short_description": "A sleek, functional backpack designed for everyday use.",
              "tags": ["Bag"],
              "model": "Founder's Edition",
              "asin_amazon_standard_identification_number": "B000139Q",
              "country_of_origin": "Spain",
              "vendor": "Plytix",
              "published": true,
              "published_scope": "web",
              "gender": ["Unisex"],
              "main_image": ["6a38f5f6c3d65a5f868b1912"],
              "model_shots": ["6a38f5e1c3d65a5f868b18a7", "6a38f5e1c3d65a5f868b18a5"],
              "product_classification": "Bag",
              "google_shopping_completeness": 100.0,
              "website_ready": 100.0,
              "spanish_content_completeness": 100.0,
              "english_content_completeness": 100.0,
              "marketing_ready": 100.0,
              "shopify_ready": 72.73,
              "basic_information_completeness": 100.0,
              "product_specifications_completeness": 71.43,
              "amazon_ready": 81.82
            },
            "thumbnail_id": "6a38f5f2c3d65a5f868b1900",
            "parent_id": null,
            "num_variations": 0,
            "product_level": 0,
            "product_family_id": "6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186e",
            "overwritten_attributes": [],
            "product_relationships": [
              {
                "relationship_id": "6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186c",
                "relationship_label": "related_products",
                "related_products": [
                  {"quantity": 1, "last_modified": "2025-02-26T14:45:06.971000"},
                  {"quantity": 1, "last_modified": "2025-02-26T14:45:06.971000"}
                ]
              }
            ],
            "created": "2026-06-22T08:45:08.648000",
            "modified": "2026-06-22T08:45:11.161000"
          },
          "errors": []
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}

example {
  name: 422 - SKU as Identifier
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-07. Passing a SKU as {identifier} instead of a Mongo ObjectId returns 422. Contradicts API V3.md's identifier-flexibility claim. Confirmed on both pre-existing products and self-created ones.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/BAG-10157
    method: get
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 422
      text: Unprocessable Entity
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": [],
          "errors": [
            {
              "name": "ValidationError",
              "description": "Id must be of type PydanticObjectId"
            }
          ]
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}

example {
  name: 404 - Not Found After Delete
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-23. Immediately after DELETE /products/{id} (see "Delete Product.bru"'s "204 - Standard Delete" example, same product id) returns 204, a follow-up GET on the same id returns 404 -- genuine hard delete, not soft-delete. Matches the "Get X by ID - After Delete" pattern used by every other resource folder in this collection.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6ab41b6debb26b08b2285ef2
    method: get
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 404
      text: Not Found
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": [],
          "errors": [
            {
              "name": "NotFoundError",
              "description": "Document with id 6ab41b6debb26b08b2285ef2 not found"
            }
          ]
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
